we have to talk about this talking point  being used by some  on the right and even some on the left  about so-called  outside protesters in the civil unrest  that we're seeing across the country  right now in dozens of urban areas  so i'm going to tell you what's being  said i'm going to tell you  the truth and then i'm going to tell you  what the most important  takeaway is from all of this which is  that it doesn't actually matter if  protesters are coming  from so-called the outside so the right  regularly talks about so-called outside  protesters  because their priority is to make it  seem like there's not really a concern  in our country about police brutality  in the communities where the protests  are happening so when the right insists  the protesters are coming from outside  the city from somewhere else  they can disqualify and ignore the  activism  uh that is actually motivating  protesters and they can say you know  these are provocateurs  these are looters and writers burglars  people who are there for nefarious  reasons but most importantly what they  want to argue by saying  people are coming in from outside the  cities is they want to argue that not  that many people are actually concerned  about police brutality  which was the catalyst for these  protests you've also got a little slice  of the left  where you've seen the outside protesters  talking point used in the context of  framing outsiders as  the right wing astro turf whether it's  white nationalists pretending to be  antifa or  in order to make antifa and protesters  kind of amorphous or look bad or  whatever else you're seeing less of it  but it's still also not the best thing  because here's the truth  number one in terms of those getting  arrested the reports that we've seen  are that most of the arrests are people  who live in the cities or metro areas  where the protests are happening so in  minnesota there was a report about  20 percent of the arrests were outsiders  in detroit  similar numbers about 80 percent of the  arrests were from the detroit metro area  so first of all it is factually untrue  that a huge number at least of the  troublemakers are from outside areas  but here are the two really important  takeaways first of all  it would make sense that the  troublemakers  politics aside are from the outside  why well there's that phrase about dogs  don't  go to the bathroom where they eat or  something like that intuitively  of course people are more likely to  cause trouble  and destroy stuff where they don't live  it says nothing about  motive or justification for the protests  whatsoever  but it makes sense that you're more  likely to destroy a place  that you don't actually live in but  here's the most important takeaway  who cares if they're coming from the  outside you can still care about police  brutality  if you come into a city from somewhere  else mass rallies happen in urban  centers and population centers  protests happen where there are people  if you live in  northern rural indiana there aren't any  sizeable protests there because there's  such little population density it makes  perfect sense  that you would head into chicago for  example to be part of a larger protest  it doesn't mean you don't care about  police brutality it doesn't mean you're  there to commit a crime or to attack  people or to loot it just means  people are protesting where there are  people so i was reminded of this debate  that happened um around barack obama  this is he really a christian or is he  some kind of secret muslim  and at some point colin powell came out  and said  the question of whether he is or is not  muslim  is irrelevant muslims can still be  president it would change nothing about  his policies  if he were muslim and in the same way  here there is nothing invalid  about protesters from out of town much  of this country is very rural the  protests are limited to a few dozen  large urban areas  of course you're going to have people  from different places  in cities protesting because that's  where the protests are taking place  so we've got to take back this talking  point  we can't allow the right or the left to  miss the forest for the trees just  arguing about the protesters are from in  or out of the city the looters are from  in or out of the city  people are going to cities because  that's where the protests are happening  and there's nothing wrong with that
 

